\section{Exact closed forms and the second-order parity split}
\label{sec:closed}

\begin{theorem}[the two-wolf game, solved]\label{thm:twowolves}
For every population $n \ge 2$,
\[
  p_2(n) =
  \begin{cases}
    2\,s(n), & n \text{ even},\\[4pt]
    2\,s(n) - \dfrac{1}{n}, & n \text{ odd}.
  \end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both statements are proved by induction along the population ladder, using the recursion
for $F$, the exact one-wolf formula of Theorem~\ref{thm:onewolf} and
$s(n) = s(n-2)(n-1)/n$. The bases are $F(0,2) = 1 = 2s(2)$ and
$F(1,2) = 2/3 = 2s(3)-1/3$; each step reduces, after clearing denominators, to a
polynomial identity.
\end{proof}

Thus for two wolves at an \emph{even} population the union bound is attained exactly
($D_2 = 0$), while at an \emph{odd} population it is missed by exactly $1/n$.

\begin{theorem}[three and four wolves]\label{thm:threefour}
For every admissible population,
\[
  p_3(n) = 3\,s(n) - \frac{3}{n} \ \ (n \text{ odd}), \qquad
  p_3(n) = \frac{3n-4}{n-1}\,s(n) \ \ (n \text{ even}),
\]
\[
  p_4(n) = \frac{4n-8}{n-1}\,s(n) \ \ (n \text{ even}).
\]
The even prefactors are rational functions of $n$ increasing to $3$ and $4$, so for
$k \ge 3$ the union bound is approached but never attained.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[second-order parity split]\label{thm:secondorder}
With $D_k(n) = k\,s(n) - p_k(n)$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
  \text{wolves } k & n \text{ even} & n \text{ odd}\\\hline
  2 & n\,D_2(n) = 0 & n\,D_2(n) = 1\\
  3 & n\,D_3(n) = s(n-2) \to 0 & n\,D_3(n) = 3\\
  4 & n\,D_4(n) = 4\,s(n-2) \to 0 & \text{---}
\end{array}
\]
In particular the scaled defect is a rational constant on the odd fibre and a
Wallis-valued, $\Theta(n^{-1/2})$, quantity on the even fibre.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute the closed forms of Theorems~\ref{thm:twowolves} and \ref{thm:threefour} into
Definition~\ref{def:defect}. For instance at even $n$ with $k=3$,
\[
  D_3(n) = 3s(n) - \frac{3n-4}{n-1}s(n) = s(n)\frac{3(n-1)-(3n-4)}{n-1}
         = \frac{s(n)}{n-1} = \frac{s(n-2)}{n},
\]
using $s(n)/(n-1) = s(n-2)/n$. The even-fibre quantities tend to $0$ because $s(n) \to 0$
like $n^{-1/2}$ along either parity, by Theorems~\ref{thm:oddlim} and \ref{thm:evenlim}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact wolf-win table.} Fill $F[v][k]$ for $0 \le v \le V$, $0 \le k \le K$ by
Definition~\ref{def:F} in increasing order of $v$ and $k$; each entry costs $O(1)$ rational
operations, for a total of $O(VK\,M(n))$ where $M$ is the cost of a rational multiply.

\paragraph{Survival ladder and Wallis product.} $s(n)$ and $W_m$ are each computed by a
single loop of $O(n)$ rational multiplications. Theorem~\ref{thm:sq} converts between
them exactly.

\paragraph{Parity-split limit estimator.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:errorbound} the dominant
finite-$n$ error in estimating the parity constants from $\sqrt{n}\,p_k(n)$ is
$\binom{k}{2}n^{-1/2}$; the corrected estimator
\[
  \widehat{c}(n) = \sqrt{n}\,p_k(n) + \frac{\binom{k}{2}}{\sqrt{n}}
\]
removes it, and on the odd fibre for $k \le 3$ the correction is exact.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{A superselection rule}

Population parity is a discrete conserved quantity partitioning the state space into two
dynamically disconnected sectors. This is precisely the structure producing sublattice
oscillations in statistical mechanics: a system whose update rule preserves a
$\mathbb{Z}/2$ invariant can possess a well-defined limit \emph{within} each sector while
having no limit overall. The village game is a fully solvable instance in which both
sector limits are computable in closed form and their ratio identified exactly. Note that
randomness never touches the conserved quantity: the composition of the population is
random, but the population \emph{size} follows a deterministic $-2$ ladder.

\subsection{Consequences for model fitting}

An analyst possessing only numerical values and attempting the fit
$p_k(n) \approx c\,n^{-1/2}$ will obtain residuals alternating in sign at every step with
an amplitude that does not decay relative to the signal, since the two curves differ by a
fixed factor $\pi/2$. Absorbing this into higher-order terms is impossible, as no
asymptotic expansion in powers of $n^{-1/2}$ oscillates. Theorem~\ref{thm:main}(4) is the
formal statement of that impossibility: the correct model is \emph{two} expansions indexed
by a conserved discrete label.

\subsection{The null-model programme}

Any assertion that informed play improves the village's odds implicitly compares against a
baseline. This paper supplies that baseline to second order and shows that it depends on a
variable not normally recorded: the parity of the roster. The difference between the two
baselines is
$k\bigl(\sqrt{\pi/2}-\sqrt{2/\pi}\bigr)n^{-1/2} \approx 0.455\,k\,n^{-1/2}$, which for
$n = 25$ and $k = 3$ is roughly $27$ percentage points. Comparisons across rosters of
different parity are therefore not directly meaningful without correction.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{A rational-function ladder for odd-population defects.} Exact computation
suggests that for every $k \ge 2$ there is a rational function $R_k$, of degree
$\lceil (k-2)/2\rceil$ over degree $\lceil (k-2)/2\rceil$, with
$n\,D_k(n) = R_k(n)$ for every odd $n \ge k$ and $R_k(\infty) = \binom{k}{2}$. The first
instances are
\[
  R_2 = 1,\quad R_3 = 3,\quad R_4(n) = \frac{6n-13}{n-2},\quad
  R_5(n) = \frac{10n-25}{n-2},\quad R_6(n) = \frac{15n^2-105n+183}{(n-2)(n-4)},
\]
with limits $1,3,6,10,15 = \binom{k}{2}$. The mechanism suggested by
Theorem~\ref{thm:defectrec} is that on the odd fibre the survival-valued inhomogeneity is
absent, so the defect recursion closes inside the field of rational functions of $n$, the
degree growing by one every two steps. If true, the odd-population game is solved in
closed form for every fixed $k$, upgrading Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} from inequality to
asymptotic equality.

\paragraph{A Wallis obstruction on the even fibre.} Conversely, on the even fibre one
expects no rational description: for every $k \ge 3$,
$n\,D_k(n) = S_k(n)\,s(n-2)$ with $S_k$ rational and $S_k(\infty) = c_k > 0$, so that the
even-population defect is $\Theta(n^{-1/2})$ and is not a rational function of $n$. The
established cases are $S_2 = 0$, $S_3 = 1$, $S_4 = 4$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:secondorder}).

\paragraph{Further questions.} Does the dichotomy persist when the nightly kill is
randomised, or the day vote biased? What is the joint asymptotics when $k = k(n) \to
\infty$, e.g.\ $k \asymp \sqrt{n}$, where the union bound ceases to be sharp? Is there a
third-order term, and does it split further?

\section{Conclusion}

The information-free village game carries a conserved $\mathbb{Z}/2$ label --- the parity
of the initial population --- that the dynamics never destroys, because the population
decreases by exactly two per round whatever happens. We have shown that this label
survives into the asymptotics: for every fixed wolf count $k \ge 1$ there are two distinct
asymptotic expansions of the win probabilities, sharing the leading term but with
first-order coefficients $k\sqrt{2/\pi}$ and $k\sqrt{\pi/2}$ whose ratio is exactly
$\pi/2$, independently of $k$; consequently no single parity-blind scaling limit exists.
The dichotomy is already exact at every finite population, separated by the threshold $1$,
which is precisely the geometric mean of the two limiting constants. Quantitatively, the
union bound $k\,s(n)$ is correct to within $\binom{k}{2}/n$, an estimate attained on the
odd fibre for two and three wolves; and for two wolves the game is solved outright,
$p_2(n) = 2s(n)$ at even $n$ and $2s(n) - 1/n$ at odd $n$. The second-order structure
splits again, and this time by kind: rational on the odd fibre, Wallis-valued on the even
one.

